## Why This Is Asked
Tests branchless thinking and lane-wise masking, fundamental techniques in low-level libraries and SIMD operations.

## Key Concepts
- Lane-wise masking without conditionals using two's complement arithmetic
- 128-bit composition from 32-bit chunks
- Bitwise extraction and packing operations

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode C-like implementation demonstrating the core idea
uint128 blendMask128(uint128 x){
  uint32_t a3 = (x.hi >> 32) & 0xFFFFFFFF;
  uint32_t a2 = x.hi & 0xFFFFFFFF;
  uint32_t a1 = (x.lo >> 32) & 0xFFFFFFFF;
  uint32_t a0 = x.lo & 0xFFFFFFFF;
  uint64_t m3 = -(uint64_t)(a3 & 1);
  uint64_t m2 = -(uint64_t)(a2 & 1);
  uint64_t m1 = -(uint64_t)(a1 & 1);
  uint64_t m0 = -(uint64_t)(a0 & 1);
  uint128 result;
  result.hi = (m3 << 32) | m2;
  result.lo = (m1 << 32) | m0;
  return result;
}
```

## Quick Tests
- Input: 0x123456789ABCDEF0 → Output: 0x0000000000000000 (all lanes even)
- Input: 0x12345679_9ABCDEF1 → Output: 0xFFFFFFFF_00000000 (only A0 odd)

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to count 1-runs in a 64-bit word using bit-twiddling with no loops.

## Key Concepts
- Start mask n & ~(n << 1) identifies 1-run starts
- Parallel popcount technique to count bits
- Edge cases: all zeros, runs spanning MSB

## Code Example
```cpp
uint64_t countOneRuns64(uint64_t n){
  uint64_t starts = n & ~(n << 1);
  uint64_t x = starts;
  x = x - ((x >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  x = (x & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((x >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  x = (x + (x >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  return (x * 0x0101010101010101ULL) >> 56;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt for 128-bit operands?
- Compare this approach to a loop-based scan in terms of latency and pipeline efficiency.

## Why This Is Asked
Demonstrates mastery of fixed-popcount permutations. Tests carry propagation and ability to explain a one-shot solution without loops.

## Key Concepts
- Snoob algorithm for next with same popcount
- Carry ripple and bitwise masks
- Edge-case handling for zero or maximal permutations

## Code Example
```c
static inline uint64_t nextBitPermutation64(uint64_t x) {
    // Assumes x != 0 and not the maximal permutation for its popcount
    uint64_t smallest = x & -x;
    uint64_t ripple = x + smallest;
    uint64_t ones = x ^ ripple;
    ones = (ones >> 2) / smallest;
    return ripple | ones;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt to arbitrary bit-widths?
- Compare this approach to a loop-based generator in terms of performance and readability.

## Why This Is Asked
Tests branchless 128-bit rotation using only shifts.

## Key Concepts
- View 128-bit value via 128-bit type
- Branchless rotation with shifts and modulo
- Correct unpacking into hi/lo

## Code Example
```c
void rotateRight128(uint64_t hi, uint64_t lo, unsigned int k, uint64_t *out_hi, uint64_t *out_lo) {
  unsigned int kk = k & 127;
  __uint128_t v = (((__uint128_t)hi) << 64) | lo;
  v = (v >> kk) | (v << ((128 - kk) % 128));
  *out_hi = (uint64_t)(v >> 64);
  *out_lo = (uint64_t)v;
}
```

